.TH "PERFORMANCESERVERTIMING.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceServerTiming.toJSON \- PerformanceServerTiming: toJSON()
method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]PerformanceServerTiming\f[R] interface returns a
JSON\-serializable plain object representing the
\f[CR]PerformanceServerTiming\f[R] object.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]PerformanceServerTiming\f[R] object is encountered during JSON
serialization.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A JSON\-serializable plain object containing the following properties:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]name\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]duration\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]description\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example obtains a \f[CR]PerformanceServerTiming\f[R] object.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a plain object.
Its properties have the same values as the corresponding properties of
the original object.
.PP
Server timing metrics require the server to send the
\f[CR]Server\-Timing\f[R] header.
For example:
.IP
.EX
Server\-Timing: cache;desc=\(dqCache Read\(dq;dur=23.2
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]serverTiming\f[R] entries can live on \f[CR]navigation\f[R]
and \f[CR]resource\f[R] entries.
.PP
Example using a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R], which notifies of new
\f[CR]navigation\f[R] and \f[CR]resource\f[R] performance entries as
they are recorded in the browser\(cqs performance timeline.
Use the \f[CR]buffered\f[R] option to access entries from before the
observer creation.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  list.getEntries().forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    entry.serverTiming.forEach((serverTiming) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
      \f[B]const\f[R] json = serverTiming.toJSON();
      console.log(json); \f[I]// A plain object\f[R]
      console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
      console.log(json.name); \f[I]// Same value as serverTiming.name\f[R]
    });
  });
});

[\(dqnavigation\(dq, \(dqresource\(dq].forEach((type) \f[B]=>\f[R]
  observer.observe({ type, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] }),
);
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
Within the callback from the previous example, the same object can be
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(serverTiming));
.EE
.PP
This would log a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqname\(dq: \(dqcache\(dq,
  \(dqduration\(dq: 23.2,
  \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqCache Read\(dq
}
.EE
